    {
      "type": "examAlert",
      "items": [
        "S-waves cannot travel through liquids, creating a massive shadow zone beyond 105° from the epicenter covering over 40% of the Earth's surface.",
        "The P-wave shadow zone forms a distinct band between 105° and 140° from the epicenter due to refraction at the core-mantle boundary.",
        "P-waves resemble sound waves with particle movement parallel to wave propagation, whereas S-waves move particles perpendicular to the direction of wave travel."
      ],
      "id": "uhfa1nl"
    },
    {
      "type": "keyFacts",
      "title": "Key Takeaways",
      "items": [
        "Seismic waves are energy waves generated by the sudden release of energy during an earthquake, traveling through the Earth's layers.",
        "Body waves originate at the focus and travel through the Earth's interior, consisting of Primary (P) waves and Secondary (S) waves.",
        "Primary (P) waves are compressional longitudinal waves that travel fastest and can pass through solid, liquid, and gaseous mediums.",
        "Secondary (S) waves are transverse shear waves that travel slower than P-waves and can pass exclusively through solid materials.",
        "Surface waves (L-waves and Rayleigh waves) travel along the Earth's crust, arriving last at seismographs but causing the most catastrophic damage."
      ],
      "id": "nmxgro0"
    }
